A||||11||Lewis||Oxygen sensor falling out..how can I give it a good seal..||||1||
Z||000000||Lewis||06-10-2000||11:06 PM||||Put new oxygen sensor in my car.(6cyl).  Did not have any problems getting it out it was not rusted in.  The problem I am having is that it came out twice today after I tightened it.  Can I put some sealant on it or can I use the stuff that plumbers use (that white tape)?||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-10-2000||11:37 PM||||either you got the wrong sensor or the hole is stripped. you'll have to have it repaired. I don't think there is a "helicoil" for that. no, sealer won't hold it if the hole is shot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||06-10-2000||11:50 PM||||Hmm, if it is tightening up it should have threads left. How tight is it being torqued?<P>And it should have anti-seize carefully smeared on the threads (none can be past the last thread) as coming out is opposite of the normal problem with O2 sensors.||||reg||12
Z||000003||86SE||06-11-2000||01:43 AM||||Ahhh, just weld it in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000004||Lewis||06-11-2000||08:32 AM||||There is lots of thread there.  It is not stripped.  Just seems to back out when driving.  I use to get a code for the sensor but since I put in the new one the code has dissapeared.  The car runs a lot better.  The old one use to give a strong exhaust smell and gas mileage was terrible.  I just used a wrench to tighten it down (hand torque). ||||reg||12
Z||000005||lowCG||06-11-2000||10:29 AM||||Maybe a little red,high-temp silicone would help.Works up to 1200+,as long as it's put onto a clean surface.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-11-2000||11:15 AM||||Those sensors have pipe threads, so the tighter you screw them in the tighter they hold. DANGER: tighten it too much and something will break or strip out. The manual says to torque it to 30 ft/lbs. You might try using a torque wrench next time.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodrv6||06-11-2000||01:30 PM||||DO NOT use silicone sealer anywhere around the oxygen sensor!!!  Even a small trace of silicone will contaminate the sensor and ruin it.  In fact, it's not a good idea to use any silicone sealer at all on the engine unless it is labeled "sensor safe"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000008||lowCG||06-11-2000||01:38 PM||||All the O2 sensors I've seen have a gasket like on a spark plug,and a trace of silicone there just isn't going to harm anything,as long as you don't dip the whole thing,like it's ranch dip or something.<BR>I'd try and use a torque wrench,or if you can't fit one up in there,try flexing the wrench against another bolt somewhere else on the car to get a feel for the torque value that's required.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-11-2000||03:37 PM||||bats, the O2 sensor is not NPT threaded. Wish it was, be less of a pain in some ways but would also be more prone to fusing into the bung.<P>Yeah, sounds like the thing isn't tight enough. It did come with the sealing washer didn't it? If that's missing or wrecked it won't be right either.<P>Anti sieze should only be on the threads. don't smear it on the washer. If it is on the washer or the surfaces the washer touch then clean it off.||||reg||4
Z||000010||batboy||06-11-2000||04:01 PM||||I meant the water temp sensor had pipe threads...yeah, that's what I meant. Jeez Ogre, ya gotta always prove I'm human and make mistakes don't ya...LOL.||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||06-12-2000||12:39 PM||||Just saying the word *silicone* around an O2 will contaminate it.<P>Terry||||reg||1
